6	
7	## The two moments you review
8	
9	There are exactly two:
10	
11	```
12	/opsx:propose ──► REVIEW THE PLAN ──► /opsx:apply ──► REVIEW THE CODE ──► /opsx:archive
13	                  (before any code)                    (/opsx:verify)
14	```
15	
16	1. **After `/opsx:propose`** (or `/opsx:ff`), before `/opsx:apply` — read the plan while it's still just words.
17	2. **After building**, with `/opsx:verify` — check that the code actually did what the plan said.
18	
19	The first review is the one that saves you the most, and the one people skip. This page spends most of its time there.
20	
21	## Read it in this order
22	
23	A change is a folder of plain Markdown in `openspec/changes/<name>/`. Read the files in the order that lets you quit earliest if something's wrong:
24	
25	```
26	openspec/changes/add-dark-mode/
27	├── proposal.md      1. the intent and scope   ← if this is wrong, stop here
28	├── specs/…/spec.md  2. the requirements       ← the heart of the review
29	├── design.md        (only for bigger changes) — the technical approach
30	└── tasks.md         3. the plan of work
31	```
32	
33	You don't need to read every line. You need to answer three questions, one per file.
34	
35	## The proposal: is this the right problem?
36	
37	Open `proposal.md` first. It captures the "why" and "what" — the intent, the scope, the approach in a paragraph or two.
38	
39	**What good looks like:** one clear intent, a scope you recognize, and a reason this is worth doing now.
40	
41	**Red flags:**
42	
43	- It solves a slightly *different* problem than the one you asked for.
44	- The scope has grown — you asked for a theme toggle and the proposal also touches auth "while we're in there."
45	- It's vague. "Improve the settings page" is not a scope; "add a dark-mode toggle that respects the OS preference" is.
46	
47	**The question to answer:** *Does this match what I actually asked for, and is anything sneaking in?* If the answer is no, stop — don't read further, fix the proposal (see [Pushing back](#pushing-back-is-cheap)).
48	
49	## The spec deltas: is "done" defined correctly?
50	
51	This is the heart of the review. The delta specs under `specs/` say what will be *true* when the change ships — as requirements and the scenarios that prove them:
52	
53	```markdown
54	## ADDED Requirements
55	
56	### Requirement: Dark Mode Toggle
57	The system SHALL let a user switch between light and dark themes.
58	
59	#### Scenario: Respects the OS preference on first load
60	- GIVEN a user who has never set a theme
61	- WHEN they open the app on a device set to dark mode
62	- THEN the app renders in dark mode
63	```
64	
65	**What a good requirement looks like:** one clear `SHALL`/`MUST` statement you could hand to a tester, and at least one scenario whose GIVEN/WHEN/THEN actually exercises that statement.
66	
67	**Red flags:**
68	
69	- **A vague requirement.** "The system SHALL be fast" can't be built or tested. What's fast?
70	- **A requirement with no scenario**, or a scenario that doesn't test the requirement it sits under.
71	- **The most valuable catch of all: what's missing.** The AI faithfully writes down what you *said*. Your job is to notice what you *forgot* to say. If you cared most about the OS-preference case and no scenario mentions it, that's the review paying for itself.
72	
73	Read the deltas asking *would I be happy if the system did exactly — and only — this?* Nothing here is about code yet, so it stays cheap to change.
74	
75	## The tasks: is the plan of work sane?
76	
77	Open `tasks.md` last. It's the implementation checklist the AI will work through.
78	
79	**What good looks like:** ordered steps, each traceable to a requirement, nothing mysterious.
80	
81	**Red flags:**
82	
83	- A task with no matching requirement (where did that come from?).
84	- One giant "implement the feature" task that hides all the real decisions.
85	- A task that touches something outside the scope you just approved.
86	
87	You're not estimating or micromanaging here — you're checking that the plan matches the requirements you already accepted.
88	
89	## Pushing back is cheap
90	
91	If any of the three questions came back wrong, say so. There are no phases and nothing is locked — you fix it and move on. Two ways, exactly as in [Editing a change](editing-changes.md):
92	
93	- **Edit the file yourself.** It's plain Markdown; change the scope line, tighten a requirement, delete a task.
94	- **Tell the AI what's wrong** and let it revise: *"drop the auth changes — out of scope,"* *"add a scenario for when the user has already picked a theme,"* *"split task 3 into schema and UI."*
95	
96	Then re-read the part you changed. Re-draft until it's a plan you'd sign your name to. That back-and-forth *is* the product working.
97	
98	## After the code: verify
99	
100	Once the work is built, `/opsx:verify` is your second review. It re-reads the artifacts and the code and reports mismatches across three dimensions:
101	
102	| Dimension | What it checks |
103	|-----------|----------------|
104	| **Completeness** | Every task done, every requirement implemented, scenarios covered |
105	| **Correctness** | The implementation matches the spec's intent, edge cases handled |
106	| **Coherence** | Design decisions actually show up in the code |
107	
108	```
109	You: /opsx:verify
110	
111	AI:  Verifying add-dark-mode...
112	
113	     COMPLETENESS
114	     ✓ All 8 tasks in tasks.md are checked
115	     ✓ All requirements in specs have corresponding code
116	     ⚠ Scenario "Respects the OS preference on first load" has no test coverage
117	```
118	
119	It flags issues as CRITICAL, WARNING, or SUGGESTION, and it does **not** block archiving — it surfaces the gaps and leaves the call to you. This is the difference between "did the AI write code" and "did it build what we agreed."
120	
121	`/opsx:verify` is in the expanded profile. If you don't have it, turn it on with `openspec config profile` (then `openspec update`), or just re-read the change and the diff yourself.
122	
123	## Right-size the review
124	
125	Not every change earns the full pass. A one-file typo fix deserves a twenty-second skim. A change that touches auth, payments, or data you can't recover deserves every question above. The point was never ceremony — it's spending your attention where a mistake would be expensive, and skimming where it wouldn't.
126	
127	## The two-minute checklist
128	
129	- [ ] The proposal's intent matches what I asked for.
130	- [ ] Nothing extra has crept into the scope.
131	- [ ] Every requirement is specific enough to test.
132	- [ ] Every requirement has a scenario that actually exercises it.
133	- [ ] The case I care about most is covered.
134	- [ ] Tasks map to requirements; nothing is mysterious or out of scope.
135	- [ ] I'd be comfortable if the AI built exactly this and nothing more.
136	
137	If all seven pass, run `/opsx:apply` with confidence. If any fail, that's not a setback — it's the two minutes doing its job.
